sections.s2_technical:
You create it with a count: `CountDownLatch gate = new CountDownLatch(1)` for a startup gate, or `new CountDownLatch(workerCount)` for a completion gate. `await()` blocks the caller until the count reaches zero; if it is already zero, await returns immediately. `countDown()` decrements the count by one and never blocks; extra calls after zero are harmless no-ops.

Startup gate pattern: every worker does `startGate.await()` at the top of its task, and the coordinator does `startGate.countDown()` once when it is ready. All workers unblock together, which is how you benchmark or launch simultaneously.

```java
CountDownLatch startGate = new CountDownLatch(1);
for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++)
        pool.submit(() -> { startGate.await(); doSettlement();
        return null; });
startGate.countDown();   // fire the starting gun — all 10 begin together
```

Completion gate pattern: the count equals the number of workers, each worker calls `countDown()` in a finally block when it finishes, and the coordinator calls `doneGate.await()` to wait for all of them.

```java
CountDownLatch doneGate = new CountDownLatch(10);
for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++)
    pool.submit(() -> { try { doSettlement(); } finally { doneGate.countDown(); } });
doneGate.await();        // coordinator blocks until all 10 finished
```

There is a timed `await(long, TimeUnit)` returning a boolean, which you use so the coordinator does not hang forever if a worker dies; if it returns false you raise an alert. Put `countDown()` in a finally block so a crashing worker still decrements, otherwise the coordinator waits forever.

The crucial limitation: a latch is one-shot. After it reaches zero it cannot go back up. If you need threads to meet repeatedly at the end of each round, a `CyclicBarrier` resets itself automatically each cycle, and a `Phaser` adds dynamic registration and multiple phases. So a latch is for a single event; a barrier is for repeated rendezvous.

